  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35587</id>
    <title>Property Taxes and Housing Allocation Under Financial Constraints</title>
    <updated>2026-08-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Joshua Coven, Sebastian Golder, Arpit Gupta &amp; Abdoulaye Ndiaye</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Low property taxes amplify lock-in among elderly homeowners, limiting housing access for young families. Raising them reallocates housing toward the young through two channels: capitalization into lower prices reduces required downpayments for financially constrained buyers, a form of embedded leverage, while higher tax obligations raise holding costs for older owners. In our overlapping generations model, raising California’s property taxes to Texas levels increases young homeownership while decreasing elderly homeownership. Removing step-up basis also lowers elderly homeownership, suggesting their tenure is sustained by bequest tax advantages. The tax treatment of housing shapes housing allocation across generations.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35587"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-08-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
