    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35628</id>
    <title>Do Minimum Wages Help Worker in Poor and Low-Income Families?</title>
    <updated>2026-08-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>David Neumark &amp; Emma Wohl</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We provide the first direct estimates of the effects of minimum wages on low-wage workers in families at different points of the distribution of income-to-needs, using data from the Survey of Income and Program Participation, which oversamples low-income families. We find adverse – rather than beneficial – effects of minimum wages on the employment, hours, and earnings of initially-employed low-wage workers in poor and low-income families. Although we do not find a gradient indicating more adverse effects on the poorest low-wage workers, the adverse effects for poor and low-income low-wage workers help explain why minimum wages do not reduce poverty.</content>
